---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ReleaseInfo from '../components/ReleaseInfo.astro';
import ShotStrip from '../components/ShotStrip.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Download ChordPlane for Android"
  description="Download the signed ChordPlane APK. Android 7.0 or newer, free, and updates install over the old build without touching your songs."
>
  <section class="wrap page-head">
    <h1>Get ChordPlane for Android</h1>
    <p>
      A signed APK, published straight from the project's releases. No account,
      no installer, no bundled extras — download it, tap it, play.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a
        id="download-apk"
        class="btn btn-primary"
        href="https://github.com/danny192168/ChordPlane-v2/releases/latest/download/chordplane-latest.apk"
        rel="noopener"
      >
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" width="18" height="18">
          <path
            d="M12 3v11m0 0 4.5-4.5M12 14 7.5 9.5M5 17.5V19a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h10a2 2 0 0 0 2-2v-1.5"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.8"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
        </svg>
        Download APK
      </a>
    </div>
    <ReleaseInfo />
  </section>

  <ShotStrip />

  <section id="install" class="section wrap install-grid" aria-labelledby="install-title">
    <div>
      <h2 id="install-title">Three steps</h2>
      <ol class="steps" style="margin-top: 22px">
        <li>
          <strong>Download the APK.</strong> It lands in your browser's
          downloads, like any other file.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Open it and allow the install.</strong> Android will ask once
          whether your browser may install apps. That prompt is normal for
          anything that doesn't come from Play.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Open ChordPlane.</strong> It appears in your launcher as
          <strong>ChordPlane</strong>. Later builds install straight over this
          one.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="fine-print">
        Minimum version is Android 7.0 (API 24). The APK is signed, so Android
        can verify that updates come from the same publisher.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="download-card">
      <dl class="spec" style="margin-top: 0">
        <dt>Platform</dt>
        <dd>Android 7.0+</dd>
        <dt>Price</dt>
        <dd>Free</dd>
        <dt>Updates</dt>
        <dd>Install over the old build</dd>
        <dt>Your songs</dt>
        <dd>Stay on the device</dd>
      </dl>
      <a
        class="btn btn-primary"
        style="margin-top: 20px"
        href="https://github.com/danny192168/ChordPlane-v2/releases/latest/download/chordplane-latest.apk"
        rel="noopener">Download APK</a
      >
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2 id="questions-title">Before you install</h2>
    </div>

    <ul class="points">
      <li>
        <h3>Will updating erase my songs?</h3>
        <p>
          No. Installing a newer APK replaces the app but leaves its data alone,
          so your library and set lists carry over.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Can I share a whole set list?</h3>
        <p>
          Yes. Open a set list and choose <strong>Share setlist</strong> to send
          every song as a QR code, a link or a <code>.cpl</code> file. Your
          library stays on your device; only the set you choose is shared.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Do I need an account?</h3>
        <p>
          Not to use the app. Signing in is only needed to create share links
          you can manage and delete later.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Why does Android warn me?</h3>
        <p>
          Because the file comes from GitHub rather than an app store. The
          warning is about the source, not about the app being unsafe.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Is there an iPhone version?</h3>
        <p>Not yet. The Android build is the only one published so far.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Can I verify the download?</h3>
        <p>
          Yes — the checksum link above gives you the SHA-256. Compare it with
          <code>shasum -a 256 chordplane-latest.apk</code> on a Mac or Linux
          machine.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Where do I report a problem?</h3>
        <p>
          <a href="https://github.com/danny192168/ChordPlane-v2/issues" rel="noopener"
            >Open an issue on GitHub</a
          >. Include your Android version if something misbehaves.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
